- On August 27, 2026, China CDC released autumn school infectious disease prevention guidelines. With seasonal transition increasing transmission risk in crowded campuses, the guidance identifies three priority disease types: (1) respiratory infections spread mainly by droplets, including COVID-19, influenza, tuberculosis, varicella, pertussis, measles, rubella, and mumps; (2) enteric infections spread via the fecal–oral route, notably hand, foot and mouth disease, norovirus, and rotavirus; (3) vector-borne diseases transmitted by mosquitoes, such as dengue fever, chikungunya, and fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome. Preventive measures emphasize hygiene, ventilation, and vaccination. Students and staff should wash hands before meals and after leaving or coughing/sneezing; cover nose/mouth;
- Published 28 August 2026, China’s 2025 Health Development Statistics Bulletin reports 419.34 thousand cases and 24,100 deaths from Class A–B infectious diseases, and 20.5721 million cases with 51 deaths from Class C. Among Class A–B, COVID-19 remained the most frequent disease (1,554,677 cases), down ~156,000 from 2024; AIDS was the leading cause of death (19,097). The five most incident A–B diseases—COVID-19, viral hepatitis, tuberculosis, syphilis, and gonorrhea—accounted for 94.10% of cases. Rabies deaths rose to 233. In Class C, influenza caused the most illness and deaths (41), with 20.5721 million influenza cases. New to Class B are chikungunya fever and fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome as of 1 April. Vaccination measures: from August, free influenza vaccines for rural elderly (6
- Pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) on Beijing campuses calls for vigilance as students return to school. TB spreads mainly through respiratory droplets; crowded classrooms, dormitories, and libraries facilitate transmission. Key symptoms: cough for more than two weeks, sputum, hemoptysis, fever, night sweats, fatigue, and weight loss. Suspected cases should promptly visit a TB-designated facility and provide accurate information to clinicians. If diagnosed, report to the school; those with contagious risk may require leave of absence until contagion subsides, then return with medical clearance. TB is curable with standard, full-course treatment; adherence is essential. Close contacts should be screened; latent infection may warrant preventive therapy. Prevention: proper ventilation, no spitting,
